{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Winter settled over the island like a held breath. The gales came in December, one after another, rattling the tin roof of the byre and driving the rain sideways across the fields until the ditches brimmed and the path to the shore turned to black mud. On the worst nights the girl lay awake listening to the wind moan in the chimney and thought of the seedling out there under its creel, alone in the dark. Each morning before school she pulled on her boots and ran down to check it, crouching to peer between the gaps in the wicker. The twig was always there, bare and brown and stubborn, and she would let out a breath she hadn't known she was holding. In the jotter the entries grew short: still there, still there, snow. The snow came in January, a rare, thin fall that lasted only a day, and she drew the creel with a white cap on it and the two guard stones like sleeping sheep. The big rowan stood black against the sky, its branches bare but for a few shrivelled berries the blackbirds had missed. Her grandmother, visiting from Lochboisdale, stood at the window with her tea and said that a rowan by the door kept ill luck away, and that a young one planted by a child's hand was stronger still. The girl did not know if that was true. But in February, lifting the creel to look, she found a tiny swelling at the tip of the twig, dark and tight as a closed fist. That night she wrote: it is waiting to wake."}
